<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhimsen Thapa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1775">1775</a>–<a href="/years/1839">1839</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bhimsen Thapa (1775–1839) was the chief minister (mukhtiyar) of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> from 1806 to 1837. He dominated the court during the minority of the kings, built up the army and continued the kingdom's westward expansion, which led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-nepalese-war">Anglo-Nepalese War</a> of 1814–1816 and the loss of territory under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sugauli">Treaty of Sugauli</a>. Overthrown by court rivals in 1837, he died in prison in 1839.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhimsen-thapa.jpg" alt="Bhimsen Thapa" width="320" height="469" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anand Muni Shakya · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bhimsen_Thapa_sitting.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bhimsen Thapa was born in August 1775 at Pipal Thok village in Gorkha district, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amar-singh-thapa">Amar Singh Thapa</a> and Satyarupa Maya, in a military family of the Bagale Thapa clan, which had migrated east from Jumla. He had four brothers by his mother and two by his stepmother. In 1798 his father recruited him as a bodyguard for the king. He rose to influence by helping the exiled former king Rana Bahadur Shah plan his return to power in 1804, and Rana Bahadur made him a Kaji, equivalent to a minister, in the new government.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After Rana Bahadur was assassinated in 1806, Bhimsen took the title of Mukhtiyar. As Kaji he had already sent armies west, and the Battle of Khurbura in May 1804 carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>'s border to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej River</a>. In 1805 he planned the Baisathi Haran, the seizure of tax-free land held by temple trusts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> priests without valid documents, to fill the empty treasury and pay for campaigns in the far west. A Kangra campaign followed, and by early 1809 most of the Kangra lands were taken, though the fort held out and the Nepalese army was forced back by Sansar Chand and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>.</p><p>He was among the main supporters of war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, against the advice of commanders such as Bada Kaji Amar Singh Thapa. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-nepalese-war">Anglo-Nepalese War</a> of 1814–1816 ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-sugauli">Treaty of Sugauli</a>, by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> lost almost a third of its land. Edward Gardner arrived in May 1816 as the first British Resident, and Bhimsen cut him off from life in Nepal and from the king. He also built the Thapathali bridge over the Bagmati in 1811 and, in 1824 and 1825, two Dharahara towers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Bhimsen's position weakened after Queen Tripurasundari died of cholera on 26 March 1832. King Rajendra Bikram Shah and Senior Queen Samrajya Laxmi Devi challenged him, rumour blamed him for the death of the earlier king Girvan, and the British Resident Brian Houghton Hodgson worked against him. The Pande faction, who held him responsible for the death of Damodar Pande in 1804, rose at court. He was imprisoned on a charge connected with the poisoning of an infant prince, and he died by suicide in 1839.</p><p>Nepalese tradition counts him a national hero, and historians such as Chittaranjan Nepali call him a patriotic statesman who resisted foreign suzerainty. He is also criticised for the Bhandarkhal massacre of 1806, for eliminating rivals and for concentrating power in his family.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bhimsen Thapa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Bhimsen Thapa</b> dominated the government as mukhtiyar from 1806 to 1837, and the struggles between noble factions culminated in the Kot massacre of September 1846, when Jang Bahadur Kunwar and his brothers killed dozens of leading courtiers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Gorkha District, in what is now <a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/nepal">Nepal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=28.28,84.68&amp;year=1816" aria-label="Gorkha District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6767 1128 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bhimsen Thapa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7217 1398h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7217 1398h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=28.28,84.68&amp;year=1816">What was here in 1816? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
